The number \(N\) consists of 1998 ones, so \(N = \frac{10^{1998} - 1}{9}\). Its square root is \(\sqrt{N} = \frac{1}{3}\sqrt{10^{1998} - 1} = \frac{10^{999}}{3} \sqrt{1 - 10^{-1998}}\). Expanding the square root gives \(\sqrt{N} = \frac{10^{999}}{3} - \frac{1}{6}10^{-999} - \frac{1}{24}10^{-2997} - \cdots\).

The first term \(\frac{10^{999}}{3} = 333\dots333.\overline{3}\) has 999 digits of 3 before the decimal point and infinite 3's after it. The second term is \(\frac{1}{6}10^{-999} = 0.\underbrace{00\dots0}_{999 \text{ zeros}}1666\dots\), which only affects digits from the 1000th place onward. Subtracting it yields a fractional part whose first 999 digits are 3, followed by a 1 at the 1000th place and 6's thereafter. Higher-order terms begin affecting digits beyond the 2997th place, so they do not change the 1000th digit. Therefore, the 1000th digit after the decimal point is 1.

\boxed{C}

