The number of permutations of \(1, 2, \dots, 32\) satisfying the condition that \(m \mid n \implies a_m \mid a_n\) is found by analyzing the divisibility poset. Let \(d(a) = \lfloor 32/a \rfloor\) be the number of multiples of \(a\) in the set. Since the condition implies the image of the multiples of \(a\) is contained in the multiples of \(f(a)\), injectivity forces \(d(a) = d(f(a))\). This partitions the set by \(d\)-values, forcing \(f\) to be the identity on \(1\) through \(6\), and then forcing \(f(7)=7\), \(f(8)=8\), \(f(9)=9\), \(f(10)=10\) using additional divisibility constraints.

For the \(d=2\) level \(\{11,\dots,16\}\), divisor constraints force \(f(12)=12\), \(f(14)=14\), \(f(15)=15\), \(f(16)=16\), leaving only \(\{11,13\}\) to permute (2 choices). The \(d=1\) level \(\{17,\dots,32\}\) is almost entirely forced by LCM constraints from their proper divisors, with the exceptions that \(f(22)\) and \(f(26)\) swap correspondingly to the choice on \(\{11,13\}\), and the five primes \(17, 19, 23, 29, 31\) have no proper divisors \(>1\) and can be freely permuted. This gives \(5! = 120\) permutations for each of the 2 choices, yielding \(240\) total valid permutations.

\boxed{A}

The equation \((a+bi)^n = (a-bi)^n\) can be rewritten as \(\left(\frac{a+bi}{a-bi}\right)^n = 1\). Let \(z = a+bi = re^{i\theta}\) with \(r>0\) and \(\theta \in (0, \pi/2)\). Then \(\overline{z} = a-bi = re^{-i\theta}\), so \(\frac{z}{\overline{z}} = e^{2i\theta}\). The condition becomes \(e^{2i\theta n} = 1\), which implies \(2\theta n = 2\pi k\) for some integer \(k\), so \(\theta = \frac{\pi k}{n}\). Since \(\theta \in (0, \pi/2)\), we require \(0 < k < n/2\).

For \(n=1\), no integer \(k\) satisfies \(0<k<0.5\). For \(n=2\), we need \(0<k<1\), which has no integer solution. For \(n=3\), we have \(0<k<1.5\), so \(k=1\) works, giving \(\theta = \pi/3\). Then \(\tan\theta = \frac{b}{a} = \sqrt{3}\), so \(\frac{b^2}{a^2} = 3\). Direct expansion for \(n=3\) confirms \(b(3a^2 - b^2)=0 \Rightarrow b^2/a^2 = 3\).

