The sequence \(l(y)\) satisfies \(l(2y) = l(y)\) and \(l(2y+1) = (-1)^y l(y)\) with \(l(1) = 1\). For \(y = 2k\) (\(k \ge 1\)), we have \(l(2k)l(2k+2) = l(k)l(k+1)\). For \(y = 2k+1\) (\(k \ge 1\)), we have \(l(2k+1)l(2k+3) = (-1)^k l(k) \cdot (-1)^{k+1} l(k+1) = -l(k)l(k+1)\). Hence for each \(k \ge 1\), the pair \(y=2k\) and \(y=2k+1\) gives \(f(2k) + f(2k+1) = 0\).  
The sum from \(y=1\) to \(1089\) (with \(1089\) odd) becomes \(f(1)\) plus pairs \((2,3), (4,5), \ldots, (1088,1089)\) that each sum to \(0\). Since \(f(1) = l(1)l(3) = 1 \cdot (-1) = -1\), the total sum is \(-1\).

