The condition requires \(2016 \mid t^{(y)}(k)\) for all integer polynomials \(t(x)\) and integers \(k\). For any monomial \(x^n\), its \(y\)-th derivative is \(y!\binom{n}{y}x^{n-y}\). Since \(\binom{n}{y}\) is an integer, the derivative is \(y!\) times an integer polynomial. Hence \(t^{(y)}(k)\) is always a multiple of \(y!\). Taking \(t(x)=x^y\) gives \(t^{(y)}(k)=y!\), so we need \(2016 \mid y!\).  

Factor \(2016 = 2^5 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 7\). The smallest \(y\) whose factorial contains at least \(2^5\), \(3^2\), and \(7^1\) is \(8\) (since \(7!\) has only \(2^4\), while \(8!\) has \(2^7\)).

